An instructor alerted us to an issue in Lessons where an error warning banner was displayed

“Unable to save or update item. Error was:”  Where no detailed error message is included.

I have isolated this issue to use of Safari in our local instance but have not had luck reproducing in nightly servers.  See attached video for UI representation of the issue.

The instructor’s update is saved correctly but the UI error banner (see image / video ) that appears a step or two later is disorienting. 

I suspect that the update is queued in Hibernate and an error is not caught in this section of code. Later when hibernate commits, an error occurs and  triggers the UI banner at an unexpected time with no detailed message.

I believe the error (once I trapped in Dev revised code by forcing a hibernate flush) is due to: *StaleStateException on update due to optimistic locking failure (log entry update not committed)*

In:  /lesssonbuilder/components/src/java/org/sakaiproject/lessonbuildertool/model/SimplePageToolDaoImpl.java



{code:java}try {
    if(!(o instanceof SimplePageLogEntry)) {
	   getHibernateTemplate().merge(o);
    }else {
	 SimplePageLogEntry entry = (SimplePageLogEntry) o;
	 boolean isLoggedIn = StringUtils.isNotBlank(entry.getUserId());
	 if (isLoggedIn) {
	     // Updating seems to always update the timestamp on the log correctly,
           // while merging doesn't always get it right.  However, it's possible that
	     // update will fail, so we do both, in order of preference.
	     try {
		   getHibernateTemplate().update(o);
	     } catch (DataAccessException ex) {
		 log.warn("Wasn't able to update log entry, timing might be a bit off.");
		 getHibernateTemplate().merge(o);
	     }
     }
}
{code}



For diagnostic purposes I changed to forced a flush after the update to trap the error:



{code:java}try {
    if (!(o instanceof SimplePageLogEntry)) {
	    getHibernateTemplate().merge(o);  // unchanged
    } else {
	  SimplePageLogEntry entry = (SimplePageLogEntry) o;
	  boolean isLoggedIn = StringUtils.isNotBlank(entry.getUserId());
        if (isLoggedIn) {
		try {
		    getHibernateTemplate().update(o);  // original path
		    getHibernateTemplate().flush();   // ✅ Force the SQL now
		} catch (Exception ex) {  // ✅ Catch broader for full trace
		    log.warn("Wasn't able to update log entry, timing might be a bit off.", ex);

			// Optional: fallback to merge
			try {
			    getHibernateTemplate().merge(o);
			    getHibernateTemplate().flush();  // also flush this
			} catch (Exception ex2) {
			    log.error("Fallback merge also failed", ex2);
			}
		    }
		 }
	 }
{code}



I was able to capture the stack trace - see attached.  The error is :



{code:none}HibernateOptimisticLockingFailureException: Batch update returned unexpected row count from update [0]; actual row count: 0; expected: 1; statement executed: update lesson_builder_log set lastViewed=?, itemId=?, userId=?, firstViewed=?, complete=?, dummy=?, path=?, toolId=?, studentPageId=? where id=? and lastViewed=?; nested exception is org.hibernate.StaleStateException: Batch update returned unexpected row count from update [0]; actual row count: 0; expected: 1; statement executed: update lesson_builder_log set lastViewed=?, itemId=?, userId=?, firstViewed=?, complete=?, dummy=?, path=?, toolId=?, studentPageId=? where id=? and lastViewed=?

. . .

Caused by: org.hibernate.StaleStateException: Batch update returned unexpected row count from update [0]; actual row count: 0; expected: 1; statement executed: update lesson_builder_log set lastViewed=?, itemId=?, userId=?, firstViewed=?, complete=?, dummy=?, path=?, toolId=?, studentPageId=? where id=? and lastViewed=?
{code}



Next I changed the logic in my Dev instance to only do a merge versus update then merge. Doing a merge seems to have resolved the Safari issue.

{code:java}try {
    if(!(o instanceof SimplePageLogEntry)) {
	  getHibernateTemplate().merge(o);
    }else {
	  SimplePageLogEntry entry = (SimplePageLogEntry) o;
	  boolean isLoggedIn = StringUtils.isNotBlank(entry.getUserId());
	  if (isLoggedIn) {
		// Updating seems to always update the timestamp on the log correctly,
		// while merging doesn't always get it right.  However, it's possible that
		// update will fail, so we do both, in order of preference.
		try {
			getHibernateTemplate().merge(o);
		 } catch (DataAccessException ex) {
		     log.warn("Wasn't able to merge log entry, timing might be a bit off.");
		 }
	     }
       }
{code}



This may be limited to Safari due to (per AI) its aggressive approach:

 *1. Safari Caches the Page or Ajax Response Aggressively*

* Safari is notorious for caching aggressively, especially POST/PUT responses and even JavaScript XHR responses.
* It could be sending stale data — e.g., an outdated version of a {{SimplePageLogEntry}}, causing Hibernate to choke with {{StaleObjectStateException}} (even when merged, depending on your session flush behavior and version fields).

h4. *2. Race Conditions*

* If Safari is sending multiple parallel requests (e.g., due to auto-refresh, JS triggers, or quick tab changes), it may be submitting out-of-sequence updates.
* Merge helps here — it reconciles changes — but still may lose updates if timestamps or versions don’t align.

h4. 

If anyone has seen this Safari based issue in LessonBuilder, have insight, or a comment on a my Dev solution to use merge instead of update/merge fallback, your input is appreciated.
Thank you

