{noformat}06-jul-2026 10:48:08.422 [33mWARN[m [pool-13-thread-5] o.s.c.i.BaseContentService.copyAttachment Cannot add new attachment with id = /group/GRA_14812_2025/2022-23-Fundamentos Matemáticos de la IC1/attachments/7efd14ed-dfdd-4a93-863c-59d67e6e14b0/Problemas Tema 3 GIC 2022-2023.pdf, org.sakaiproject.exception.InconsistentException id: MFL_GRA_14812_2025{noformat}

I haven't found a way to reproduce it (yet) with new cases. It seems to me that the issue occurs with sites created prior to version 25 (or before some change in v25), and now referenced attachments (at least in assignments) are no longer under “content/group/” but under “content/attachment/”, which works fine and doesn't cause any issues.

When rubric grading is used with an Assignment, Gradebook item, or Test & Quizzes question, deleting the associated item and then deleting the rubric removes the {{rbc_rubric}} and {{rbc_criterion}} records but leaves associated records in {{rbc_criterion_outcome}} and {{rbc_returned_criterion_out}}.

The remaining rows reference {{criterion_id}} values that no longer exist and appear to be orphaned records.

This was reproduced on a clean Sakai 25 installation using {{auto.ddl=true}}

h3. Impact

GradingService is stateful even though it should be stateless. This makes reuse of the same service instance unsafe and fragile.

Users are impacted because:

* calculated questions are processed more times than needed
* the rendering path can depend on shared mutable state
* concurrent requests in the same session can interleave updates and cause incorrect results
* a formula parsing error can crash instead of showing the friendly error message

h3. Expected behaviour

GradingService should not keep mutable instance state.

It should:

* use local maps and local text values instead of shared fields
* safely support reuse of the same instance
* render calculated questions without relying on shared scratch state
* show the intended {{calc.extract_text_error}} message when a formula cannot be parsed

h3. Actual behaviour

GradingService has five mutable instance fields used as scratch space:

{code:java}[user] [user] private List<String> texts;                                   // no initializer; null until set
[user] [user] private HashMap<Integer,String> answersMap = new HashMap<>();
[user] [user] private LinkedHashMap<String,String> answersMapValues = new LinkedHashMap<>();
[user] [user] private LinkedHashMap<String,String> globalanswersMapValues = new LinkedHashMap<>();
[user] [user] private LinkedHashMap<String,String> mainvariablesWithValues = new LinkedHashMap<>();{code}

Current behaviour includes:

* {{populateCalculatedQuestion}} writes {{setTexts(...)}}, then another call clears the shared maps and writes {{setTexts(...)}} again
* the method later reads {{getTexts()}} and depends on the earlier value still being present
* {{extractCalcQAnswersArray}} runs three times for every calculated question rendered
* {{DeliveryActionListener}} holds one {{GradingService}} in a {{final}} field shared across every question it processes
* concurrent requests on one session can interleave {{clear()}} / {{putAll()}} on the same maps
* when a formula cannot be parsed, this code throws {{UnsupportedOperationException}}:

{code:java}if (texts.get(0).isEmpty()) {
    log.error("Unable to extract any question text from calculated question with item id {} ...");
    texts.set(0, Collections.singletonList(rb.get("calc.extract_text_error").toString()));  // UnsupportedOperationException
}{code}

The friendly {{calc.extract_text_error}} message is not shown.

h3. Other information

Introduced by:

* {{8271441253b}} on 2021-07-20, {{SAK-45737 — SAMIGO: Improving performance on calculated questions}}
* {{f89d5940d57}} on 2021-08-02, {{SAK-45737-2 — same title}}
* {{8f7c962aae4}} on 2023-07-21, {{S2U-12 (#11737) — Improving i18n and feedback on calculated questions}}
* {{39a9a433ff7}} on 2023-10-25, {{S2U-11 (#12027) — Global variables in calculated questions}}

Background, not a cause:

* {{3e0ae268416}} on 2020-04-03, {{SAK-43515, Reduce the number of times calculated questions are processed}}

Scope notes:

* {{extractCalcQAnswersArray}} already takes all four maps as parameters and fills them. It does not need a signature change.
* Four callers already pass local maps and are the pattern to copy:
** {{StatisticsService.java:1127-1128}}
** {{HistogramListener.java:1988-1991, 2003, 2065}}
** {{QuestionScoreListener.java:619-622, 626}}
** {{QuestionPoolBean.java:1289-1292, 1297, 1300}}

There is also a separate defect in the same block:

* {{extractCalcQAnswersArray}} returns {{List.of(instructionSegments, ...)}}, which is immutable
* {{populateCalculatedQuestion}} then calls {{texts.set(0, Collections.singletonList(rb.get("calc.extract_text_error").toString()));}}
* this causes {{UnsupportedOperationException}}

Provenance for that separate defect:

* {{SAK-46003}} ({{78428b36b9c}}, "Samigo ArrayIndexOutOfBounds when calculated question cannot be parsed", #9582)
* {{S2U-12}} ({{8f7c962aae4}}) later changed the return to immutable {{List.of(...)}} and turned the reassignment into {{set(0, ...)}}

Idempotency note:

* Collapsing the redundant extractions is safe
* Both {{extractCalcQAnswersArray:3262-3263}} and {{replaceSolutionOnFeedbackWithNumbers:3298-3301}} write the original template back as feedback {{text}}
* {{getCorrectItemFeedback()}} reads {{text}} ({{ItemData:517}})

The polls_migrate_jpa() procedure in sakai-reference (docs/conversion/sakai_26_mysql_conversion.sql) converts the legacy Polls schema (numeric POLL_ID) to the new JPA/UUID schema. As part of that process it deletes any POLL_OPTION rows that belong to a poll whose POLL_UUID could not be resolved:

{{DELETE FROM POLL_OPTION WHERE OPTION_POLL_ID_TMP IS NULL;}}

Later, the procedure cleans up POLL_VOTE, but only removes votes with a null option reference:

{{DELETE FROM POLL_VOTE WHERE VOTE_OPTION IS NULL;}}

It does not remove votes whose VOTE_OPTION still points to a numeric OPTION_ID that was just deleted from POLL_OPTION earlier in the same procedure. Those votes survive the migration as orphans referencing a now non-existent option, silently breaking referential integrity.

Confirmed against a real production database (2,610 polls). After running the current procedure, the standard orphan-check query:

{{SELECT COUNT(*) FROM POLL_VOTE v LEFT JOIN POLL_OPTION o ON v.VOTE_OPTION = o.OPTION_ID WHERE o.OPTION_ID IS NULL;}}

returned 1036 orphaned votes, while the equivalent checks for POLL_POLL and POLL_OPTION (malformed id, duplicate id, orphaned option) all returned 0 — everything else about the migration was clean, only POLL_VOTE had leftover orphans.

Suggested fix:

{{DELETE FROM POLL_VOTE
WHERE VOTE_OPTION IS NULL
   OR VOTE_OPTION NOT IN (SELECT OPTION_ID FROM POLL_OPTION);}}

This has been validated against the same production dataset: applying it before the VOTE_OPTION NOT NULL / index steps removes exactly the orphaned rows and the orphan-check query then returns 0.
